# Malformed traffic. Hand built -- the capture is a clean link coming up.
#
# A bench analyzer meets all of these. Each must produce an error field and
# stop, never a confident wrong decode, and never a read past the phase.
#
# 1. An opcode Table 2 does not define. 0Ch sits in the gap between the flash
#    opcodes and the channel-independent ones.
CMD 0C 00

# 2. A defined opcode whose packet shape has not been transcribed yet. RESET
#    is real (Table 2, p.27, "In-band RESET command") and is the last opcode
#    with no transcribed shape -- the description is one line and no figure in
#    the document draws the transaction. The decoder must say so rather than
#    guess a length.
#
#    This case used to be PUT_OOB, which was the same situation until Figure 45
#    was read. Kept pointed at whatever is still a gap, because the path it
#    exercises is "a real opcode we have not finished" and that has to stay
#    distinguishable from "not an opcode at all", which is case 1.
CMD FF 00

# 3. A good GET_CONFIGURATION command with one bit flipped in the CRC byte.
#    C9h where the capture has C8h.
CMD 21 00 20 C9
TAR
RSP 08 00 0B 00 00 0F 01 91

# 4. Chip select deasserting mid-header: the address field is one byte short
#    and there is no CRC at all.
CMD 21 00

# 5. A configuration address with the top four bits set. §3.7 p.38 says the
#    controller must drive them to zero and the target should ignore them --
#    so this transaction WORKS, reaches the Channel 1 register, and is still a
#    controller violating the specification. The decoder must say so rather
#    than mask the address off and report it as plain 0020h. Nothing else on
#    the bus will flag it.
CMD 21 F0 20 DC
TAR
RSP 08 00 0B 00 00 0F 01 91

# 6. A configuration address that is not DWord aligned. Bits [1:0] are
#    described as hard-wired to 00, so seeing 22h is an anomaly.
CMD 21 00 22 C6
TAR
RSP 08 00 00 00 00 0F 01 2E

# 7. A well formed address in a reserved range. 050h-7FFh is Reserved in
#    Table 21, so there is no register here to name bits in.
CMD 21 00 50 9F
TAR
RSP 08 00 00 00 00 0F 01 2E

# 8. A cycle type that is real -- on another channel. 21h is the OOB
#    (Tunneled SMBus) Message in Table 5, p.48, and the peripheral channel
#    defines nothing at that encoding. Table 5 note 3, p.49: "The combination
#    of command opcode and cycle type encoding must be unique. There is no
#    requirement that cycle type encodings must be unique across command
#    opcodes."
#
#    So a decoder that looks a cycle type up by its byte alone finds a
#    plausible name here and reports an SMBus message inside a peripheral
#    packet. The lookup has to be keyed by the channel the opcode came from,
#    and the error has to name which channel was consulted.
CMD 00 21 00

# 9. C1C0 = 10b, the encoding Table 2 note 1 reserves. The short cycle's data
#    length lives nowhere else, so the packet length is genuinely unknown --
#    42h is PUT_IORD_SHORT with a request length that does not exist.
CMD 42 00 20 00

# 10. The same channel-keyed lookup from the other side. 00h is Memory Read 32
#     on the peripheral channel and Flash Read on the flash channel, and the
#     OOB channel defines exactly one cycle type -- 21h. So an OOB packet
#     carrying 00h names nothing, and a byte-keyed table would confidently
#     report a memory read tunnelled inside an SMBus message.
CMD 06 00 00

# 11. An OOB message whose two length statements disagree. Section 4.2.3, p.73:
#     "The presence of SMBus PEC is determined through a simple arithmetic
#     operation between the eSPI OOB header length field and the SMBus Byte
#     Count." Length 8 minus the 3 SMBus header bytes minus a Byte Count of 2
#     leaves 3 bytes where the specification allows either nothing or one PEC
#     byte.
#
#     This is the OOB channel's version of the whole problem: the packet is a
#     perfectly well formed eSPI transaction. The OOB Length alone decides how
#     many bytes to read, so the CRC lands where it should and every other
#     field checks out. Only comparing the two counts finds it.
#
#     21     Cycle Type: OOB (Tunneled SMBus) Message
#     00 08  Tag 0, Length 008h -> 8 bytes of data
#     2C     SMBus Target Address 16h, bit 0 = 0
#     0F     SMBus Command Opcode: MCTP
#     02     SMBus Byte Count = 2      <- says 2, the Length leaves room for 5
#     AA BB CC DD EE
#
#     The transaction is completed with an ordinary ACCEPT so that the only
#     fault in it is the one being tested.
#
#     CRC bytes pasted from tools/crc_ref.py (rule R4).
CMD 06 21 00 08 2C 0F 02 AA BB CC DD EE 73
TAR
RSP 08 0F 01 95
